<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/ellicott-main-street.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/ellicott-main-street-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/ellicott-main-street.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="768" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.18"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/service-locations/">Locations</a></li><li aria-current="page">Ellicott City</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Central Maryland · Howard County</p><h1>Ellicott City Insurance Appraiser for Water and Storm Claims</h1><p class="lede">Ellicott City sits where steep stream valleys meet the Patapsco River, and its claim history reflects that. Our insurance appraiser work in Ellicott City, MD focuses on separating covered water damage from excluded flood and pricing historic repairs properly.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn brass" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ghost-l" href="/start-a-claim-review/">Request a free review</a></div><dl class="ph-facts"><div><dt>County</dt><dd>Howard County</dd></div><div><dt>Region</dt><dd>Central Maryland</dd></div><div><dt>Inspections</dt><dd>On site</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><div class="localcard"><div><h2 class="lc-h">Local claim risks</h2><ul class="chips"><li>Flash flooding</li><li>Sewer and drain backup</li><li>Historic masonry damage</li><li>Hail</li><li>Tree strikes</li></ul></div><div><h2 class="lc-h">Storms on record</h2><ul class="dots"><li>Tropical Storm Agnes, 1972</li><li>Ellicott City flash flood, July 30, 2016</li><li>Ellicott City flash flood, May 27, 2018</li></ul></div><div class="full"><h2 class="lc-h">Housing mix</h2><p>A stone and brick historic mill town along Main Street, surrounded by Columbia’s planned-community townhouses and larger suburban single-family homes across Howard County.</p><p class="hoods"><b>Neighborhoods &amp; nearby:</b> Historic Ellicott City, Columbia, Elkridge, Catonsville, Clarksville, Marriottsville, Woodstock</p></div></div><h2>A town shaped by its stream valleys</h2>
<p>Historic Ellicott City grew up as a mill town in a narrow valley where smaller streams drop toward the Patapsco River. That geography makes heavy rain dangerous, and it means an insurance appraiser in Ellicott City, MD has to understand the difference between surface flooding, which homeowners policies generally exclude, and water damage the policy does cover, such as a burst pipe, a roof leak or a sewer backup where that endorsement has been purchased.</p>
<h2>Floods of 2016 and 2018</h2>
<p>Flash floods tore through Main Street on July 30, 2016 and again on May 27, 2018, damaging buildings, vehicles and businesses in the historic district. Decades earlier, Tropical Storm Agnes in June 1972 brought major Patapsco River flooding to the same valley. Those events shape how carriers approach local claims. Adjusters may assume any water is flood, while owners may assume every loss is covered. The facts of each claim decide it, and the amount of covered damage is what appraisal measures.</p>
<p>Where both flood and homeowners policies apply, coordinating the two estimates matters so that items aren’t dropped between them.</p>
<h2>Historic buildings versus planned-community townhouses</h2>
<p>Howard County’s housing splits into two very different groups. Main Street’s granite and brick buildings call for masonry restoration, period trim and careful work on structures that may be subject to historic review. Columbia’s townhouses and the newer single-family homes in Clarksville, Marriottsville and Woodstock are more standard construction, but shared roofs and HOA rules add their own complications. Frequent disagreements include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Stone and lime-mortar repairs priced as modern brick and cement work</li>
<li>Sewer-backup claims capped at a low endorsement limit without a clear breakdown</li>
<li>Townhouse roof repairs that ignore how shingles and flashings tie into the neighboring unit</li>
<li>Mould remediation left off after delayed drying</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/property-claim-services/water-damage-appraisal/">water damage appraisal</a> page explains how drying, remediation and rebuild costs are usually priced.</p>
<h2>Where we work around Ellicott City</h2>
<p>Besides the historic district, we inspect in Columbia, Elkridge, Clarksville, Marriottsville and Woodstock, and across the county line in Catonsville. Hail and tree damage are common in the suburban parts of the county, especially during spring and summer thunderstorms.</p>
<p>Older homes along the steep side streets above Main Street also deal with retaining walls, stone foundations and hillside drainage. When water damage follows a storm, carriers may point to long-term seepage, so dated photos and a clear account of when water first appeared help show a sudden event rather than a slow leak.</p>
<h2>Tips for Howard County claimants</h2>
<p>Note how water entered: through the roof, up through a floor drain, or in from the street. Keep any notices from the county or utility about storm conditions. If you have both a flood policy and a homeowners policy, send each adjuster a list of what you are claiming under the other one so nothing falls through.</p>
<p>Maryland Insurance Appraiser can review the carrier’s estimate and tell you whether the covered portion was priced fairly. For questions about whether a peril is covered at all, speak with a Maryland attorney.</p><h2>Services for Ellicott City property owners</h2><p>Every service is available in Ellicott City and the rest of Howard County, including:</p><ul class="cols2"><li><a href="/property-claim-services/appraisal-demand-representation/">Appraisal Demand Representation</a></li><li><a href="/property-claim-services/home-insurance-appraisal/">Home Insurance Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-claim-services/commercial-hoa-appraisal/">Commercial &amp; HOA Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-claim-services/roof-hail-damage-appraisal/">Roof &amp; Hail Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-claim-services/windstorm-tornado-appraisal/">Windstorm &amp; Tornado Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-claim-services/hurricane-noreaster-appraisal/">Hurricane &amp; Nor’easter Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-claim-services/water-damage-appraisal/">Water Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-claim-services/fire-smoke-damage-appraisal/">Fire &amp; Smoke Damage Appraisal</a></li></ul>
<h2>Ellicott City claim questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Does my homeowners policy cover flash flooding on Main Street?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>Generally not. Surface water flooding is excluded from standard homeowners policies and is covered by a separate flood policy. Damage from wind, a roof leak or a covered sewer backup during the same storm may still be claimed under the homeowners policy.</p></div></details><details><summary>My water backup endorsement limit seems low. Can appraisal raise it?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>No. Appraisal sets the amount of loss but can’t raise a policy limit. It can, however, make sure the full covered amount up to that limit is recognized and that other covered damage isn’t lumped into the capped category.</p></div></details></div><h2>Nearby service areas</h2><div class="rel"><a href="/service-locations/baltimore/"><small>Nearby</small><span>Baltimore</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/service-locations/towson/"><small>Nearby</small><span>Towson</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/service-locations/frederick/"><small>Nearby</small><span>Frederick</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></div></article><aside class="side"><div class="side-in"><div class="box box-call"><h2 class="box-h">Talk to an appraiser</h2><p>A free first conversation about your estimate — and a straight answer on wh